---
// One city today, but the city segment ships from day one: retrofitting one
// later means redirecting every URL on the site.
//
// In production this page is never reached. Vercel checks vercel.json's
// `redirects` BEFORE it looks at the filesystem, so `/` is answered with a real
// 307 and the browser is at /nyc before it has painted anything.
//
// This file is the dev-server equivalent. A static build cannot emit an HTTP
// status -- there is no server to send one -- so `Astro.redirect` compiles down
// to a `<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="2;...">` holding page, which is the
// two-second "Redirecting from / to /nyc" screen. `astro dev` does serve a real
// 302 from this, which is why it stays: without it `/` 404s in development.
//
// So the redirect is declared twice, once per environment. The pair to keep in
// step is this line and the `redirects` entry in vercel.json.
//
// Temporary (307/302) rather than permanent on purpose: `/` becomes a city
// picker the day a second city lands, and a 308 would sit in browser caches
// long after that.
return Astro.redirect('/nyc', 302);
---
